REPL = """# How ocean-state replicates

Before 2026-07-26 an Ocean workspace lived on exactly one machine. `ocean-state` was one
process owning one directory: durable, and gone with the laptop. It is now a
**commutative merge machine**, in the shape ce-canvas proved.

## The rule that makes it work

Every write is an op carrying its own total-order key `(lamport, replica)`, and every
write is guarded by that key rather than by the order it arrived in. Applying the same
ops in ANY order therefore yields identical state, and each costs O(1) forever.

An ordered fold — "apply everything from the beginning, in key order" — is punished
twice on a real mesh. Reads get slower with every write ever made, and a peer's op that
sorts below yours is either dropped or forces a rebuild, which happens *most* when the
mesh is working best. That is the regression that made every ce-coord app slower with
use, and it is why the guard belongs on the write instead.

## Three op types, all order-free

- `put` replaces a whole record — last-writer-wins on the record.
- `patch` writes named fields — last-writer-wins PER FIELD, so two devices editing
  different fields of one document both survive. A `put` may not roll back a newer field
  write, whichever lands first.
- `del` buries a record. A `put` with a higher key resurrects it, so delete-then-write
  and write-then-delete agree in either order.

A write that arrives before the record it belongs to is applied, not dropped: a peer's
field edit can easily outrun the record's creation, and dropping it loses a real edit.

## How ops travel

Two mechanisms, because neither is sufficient alone. **Broadcast** on `ocean.state/ops`
is immediate and lossy — pubsub is best-effort and a sleeping device hears nothing.
**Anti-entropy** is the guarantee: peers exchange per-replica high-water marks and pull
only what is missing, so the cost tracks what changed rather than what is stored. Never
put a must-arrive requirement on a broadcast.

Only stores claiming the same **replica set** (`CE_OCEANSTATE_SET`) merge. Installing
the app twice on one mesh must not silently fuse two unrelated workspaces.

## On disk

`ops.jsonl` is the truth. `snapshot.json` makes a restart a load rather than a replay.
`records/<kind>/<id>.json` remains the materialized view — one small write per save, and
a store you can still read with `cat`. Replay is idempotent (the machine rejects an op
it has already folded, by its own stamp), so recovery needs no cursor, and a cursor is
one more thing that can be wrong after a crash.

A store that predates replication is **adopted** as ops on first start: the 790 records
here are a real workspace, and they are now in the log and able to reach a second device.

## What it costs

Measured on the live 800-record workspace after the fixes below: **a write is a median
95ms** (52-203ms), against 45-70ms before replication existed. Replication roughly
doubles a write; it does not change its order of magnitude.

Getting there needed two things off the critical path, and both were found by measuring
rather than reasoning:

- The **snapshot** serializes the whole state — 3.6s here — and ran inside the write
  lock, so one write in every N took seconds and every other writer queued behind it. It
  is an optimization for restart time, so it belongs in the background, coalesced.
- The **broadcast** published inline, which is an HTTP round trip to the node: 500ms to
  3s per write against 2ms of real work. Best-effort has to mean OFF the critical path,
  not merely wrapped in a try. Ops now go to a bounded outbox; if it ever fills, dropping
  a broadcast is safe and self-healing because anti-entropy pulls it, whereas blocking
  the writer is neither.

## Proven, not asserted

- 200 shuffled delivery orders of a mixed op set — concurrent same-field writes, a patch
  outrunning its record, a delete racing a write, a resurrection above a grave — fold to
  byte-identical state.
- A control machine WITHOUT the key guard demonstrably fails that test, so the test is
  not decorative.
- 20,000 ops leave read cost unchanged.
- Two stores writing independently converge through `sync`, with both devices' field
  edits surviving.
- The 12 pre-existing tests pass untouched: the wire did not change, so the logic app
  needed no edit.

## What this does not fix

Blocks live inside a document record, so two people editing DIFFERENT blocks of the same
page still resolve at the record level. Real per-block concurrency needs blocks to be
records of their own — an Ocean modelling decision, not a storage one.

Parent: [[Ocean and ce-drive — two stores, one workspace]]
"""

